1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gards- og ættesoga for Gjestal page 157 Madland (23c), Høyland Gards- og ættesoge page 152 Foss-Eikeland (32x), Stavanger Borgerbog page 294.
2 CONT
2 CONT To America.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/69574017/jens-tormodsen-madland
2 CONT
2 CONT Jens Tormodsen Madland BIRTH	4 Oct 1804 Gjesdal kommune, Rogaland fylke, Norway DEATH	29 Nov 1885 (aged 81) Sauk Centre, Stearns County, Minnesota, USA BURIAL	 Oakland Cemetery Sauk Centre, Stearns County, Minnesota, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT JENS MADLAND
2 CONT
2 CONT In this village on Saturday, evening, November 29th, Jens Madland, in the 81st year of his age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Madland was born in Stavanger, Norway, where he attained considerable distinction, and at one time represented his district in the legislature. He was well educated, and a great reader, devoting his time very largely to genera
2 CONC l history.
2 CONT
2 CONT He came to this country in 1859, and removed to Sauk Centre thirteen years ago. He was the father of twelve children, six of whom survive him, and five residents of this state.
2 CONT
2 CONT He took a lively interest in political affairs, casting his first vote in America for Abraham Lincoln, and he has since been an enthusiastic member of the republican party.
2 CONT
2 CONT A devoted member of the Lutheran church, he has given liberally of his time and means in building up the cause of his Master, and died in confidence of a triumphant life beyond.
2 CONT
2 CONT His death, while not unexpected, was keenly felt among a large circle of friends, the blow falling with special force upon his aged wife, who has the sympathy of the entire community. He has not been a great sufferer, but faded aw
2 CONC ay, stricken in the years. Having exceeded the fullest extent allotted to this earthly pilgrimage. He was buried on Monday, Rev. O. P. Ogan officiating.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sauk Centre Herald December 4, 1884
2 CONT
2 CONT Sauk Centre Area Historical Society
2 CONT
2 CONT DIED:
2 CONT
2 CONT MADLAND—In Sauk Centre on Sabbath evening, June22, Olav(?) H. madland, sond of John D. and Annie O. Madland, age five years and five days.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a bright and promising child, beloved by his parents and friends. He suffered long and patiently and is at rest. Mr. and Mrs. Madland have the sympathy of the entire community in this great bereavement.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sauk Centre Herald June 26, 1884
2 CONT
2 CONT Sauk Centre Area Historical Society
2 CONT
2 CONT DIED
2 CONT
2 CONT MADLAND—In this city at the residence of her daughter, Mrs. Andrew Nelson, on Monday, August 28th, Mrs. Guner Madland, aged eighty-three yeas and seven months.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Madland was probably the most advanced in age of all the residents of this city, and retained her faculties in a wonderful degree until the very last. Upon the day preceding her death she held a pleasant and animated conversa
2 CONC tion with her son John, who came down from Ashby to see her, and who remained until after her burial on Tuesday. She was a remarkably bright and amiable person, and was universally esteemed and respected for her Christian virtues.
2 CONC  For nine years she has been a widow. She was born and married in Norway, coming to this country in 1859, when she settled with her family in Fillmore county, removing to Sauk Centre in 1871. She was the mother of twelve children,
2 CONC  six of whom are surviving, and three of them, Marcus, John and Mrs. Nelson, well-known here. During the past year, she has been practically helpless, but all that a loving heart and tender hands could do her for comfort and happi
2 CONC ness has been done by her daughter, with whom she made her home and family, and in her last days she has been tenderly cared for.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sauk Centre Herald August 31, 1893
2 CONT
2 CONT Sauk Centre Area Historical Society
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Theodore Jensen Madland: Theodore was a bachelor and died in his thirites after a long seige of tuberculosis. As he grew weaker he took a ring from his finger and gave it to his mother saying, "I won't need it much longe
2 CONC r." He died and was buried in Sauk Centre, Minnesota. He had been very well liked in his life and was "refined," in the terminology of his generation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Could Theodore be buried here? Since he died in 1872, he would possibly have been moved from Greenvale.
2 CONT
2 CONT Theodore Jensen Madland (son of Jens Tormodsen Madland and Gonner Thomadsdatter) was born 25 Nov 1840 in Norway, and died 08 Apr 1872 in Sauk Centre, Stearns, MN.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: The Wright, Odegaarden, Deutsch, Madland Families Page http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/w/r/i/Matthew-Francis-Wright/index.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Jens, when he was 21, inherited his father's house and blacksmith shop in Stavanger at the time Tormud left for America on The Sloop. He married, had 12 children, and became the proprietor of a brass foundry. However, he was an ob
2 CONC liging, good-natured man and often signed notes for friends or went bond for someone and when they failed, his own property was seized by the creditors. In fact, the latter became so voracious, they even threatened to take the flo
2 CONC ur the Madlands had for baking. This evoked Gonner's classic protest: "You'd better take the baby, too, if you're going to take the flour."
2 CONT
2 CONT Jen's oldest son, Thomas went to America prior to December 1856; and in some manner Jens collected funds to follow him in 1858 or 1859 with seven children. One of these, John, 8, almost fell overboard on the way. In spite of being
2 CONC  bankrupt the family somehow got to the Slooper stronghold at Fox River, Illinois. From here they went to Wisconsin, where Jens worked as a blacksmith; thence to Newburg in Fillmore County, southeastern Minnesota. From here they m
2 CONC oved to Rushford (same county), where Jens had a blacksmith shop; and finally in 1876 to Sauk Centre (Stearns County) in the west-central part of the state.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jens Tormodsen MADLAND was born in 1804 in Madland, Gjesdal, Stavanger Amt (Rogaland), Norway. He died in 1885 in Sauk Centre, Minnesota. He was buried in Lot 71, Oakland Cemetery, MN. IMMIGRANT Remained in Norway when parents and
2 CONC  3 youngest sisters sailed on the Restoration. "...when he was 21, inherited his father's house and blacksmith shop in Stavanger (Norway) at the time Tormod left for America on the Sloop. When Jens as 28 he married Gonner, daughte
2 CONC r of Thomads Marcussen of Naes in Flekkefjord*, and they acquired a family of 12 children. Meanwhile Jens prospered and became the proprietor of a brass foundry. However he was an obliging, good-natured man and often signed notes 
2 CONC for friends or went bond for someone and when they failed, his own property was seized by the creditors. ... Jens' oldest son ... went to America prior to December 1856; and in some manner Jens collected funds to follow him in 185
2 CONC 8 or 1859 with 7 children ... the family somehow got to the Slooper stronghold at Fox River, Illinois. From here they went to Wisconsin ... thence to southeastern Minnesota ... and finally in 1876 to Sauk Centre in the west-centra
2 CONC l part of the state.... In America Jens was a farmer as well as a blacksmith." (Slooper book) Contrary to the Slooper book, and apparently an error, the "Emigrants from Stavanger" Internet web site (http://www.hist.uib.no/arkivver
2 CONC ket/dnuEng.htm) indicates that he emigrated with his parents in 1825. Research by Gunleif Seldal of Norway indicates that all of the Madland children left for America with their parents aboard the sloop Restoration on 4 Jul 1825. 
2 CONC "However, when the sloop passed Tungenes outside the Byfjord the sea was rough, and some of the passengers got seasick. The master of the sloop decided then to return to Dusavik where they anchored up, awaiting the gale to subside
2 CONC . Tormod's three oldest children; Martha, Kristi and Jens were amongst those suffering from seasickness. They insisted to go ashore, and left the sloop the following morning. (According to family tradition) This was the last time 
2 CONC they ever saw their parents." (Seldal communication 4 Dec 2000)
2 CONT *Further research by Gunleif indicates that Jens' wife was not from Naes or Nes parish, Flekkefjord in Vest Agder county. She was "born in Nes (Næss) parish in Hedmark county. According to Nes church records she came to Stavanger 
2 CONC in 1830." --http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/w/r/i/Matthew-Francis-Wright-MN/WEBSITE-0001/UHP-0004.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Hannah Madland
2 CONT
2 CONT [Note: Marcus R Madland married Hannah M Gilbertson 12/24/1883]
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Hannah Madland, the beloved wife of Mark R. Madland, passed away at the Sauk Centre City Hospital March 13, 1926 after an illness of nine days of pneumonia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Madland was born at Gordemon, Norway, Oct. 14, 1865. She came to America with her parents, Mr. and Mrs. Gilbert Gilbertson in 1871. For a short time they lived near Rushford, Fillmore county, after which they moved to this se
2 CONC ction settling near Grove Lake.
2 CONT
2 CONT On Oct. 24, 1883, at Gettys Grove church Miss Gilbertson was united in marriage to Mark R. Madland. A greater part of Mr. and Mrs. Madland's married life was spent in the city of Sauk Centre, except for a few years spent on their 
2 CONC Fairy Lake farm. When she was taken ill they were living on the farm. The Herald understands that the deceased had enjoyed good health until her fatal illness. There were no children.
2 CONT
2 CONT The funeral was held from the Norwegian Lutheran church at this city Wednesday afternoon at 2:00 o'clock, conducted by Rev. Johnson, pastor of the Nor. Luth. Church at Osakis. Burial was made in Greenwood cemetery. The pallbearers
2 CONC  were S. C. Bjone, Andrew Wicklund, Sam Erickson, John Fuss, Louis Hedin and John Meyer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Madland was one of a large family of children. Her only surviving brother is Gust Gilbertson of Rushford. Mr. Gilbertson had not seen his sister for 44 years but he arrived to attend the funeral . He was accompanied by a neph
2 CONC ew of Mr. Marland's(sic) John Madland of Lewiston, Winona county.
2 CONT
2 CONT The deceased was a home loving woman and naturally her close friendships was somewhat limited. Those who knew her best pay glowing tribute to her womanly attributes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sauk Centre Herald 1926
2 CONT
2 CONT Sauk Centre Area Historical Society
2 CONT
